db/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491105 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491105 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491105 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186490972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491105 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f799ac64-6ac4-5678-be0b-90e21732933a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491109 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491109 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491109 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491109 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491109 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491109 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491109 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491109 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186490803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491109 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fceae4f2-c810-520a-9489-77125c696330" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491113 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491113 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94061" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491113 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491113 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n$$\nF=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,G=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,H=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 4 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491113 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "09cd1679-7967-5d9b-a465-697df00c6bd4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491113 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ has an associated $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $F$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}.$ Although it's a $2\\times 2$ matrix, $F$ is not symmetric since ${f}_{12}\\ne {f}_{21}.$\n\n- $G$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ since it is a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $H$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ since it's a $3\\times 3$ matrix (not $2\\times 2$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$G$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491135 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491135 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94089" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491135 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491135 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven the matrix $A$ above, evaluate the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491135 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3a0bfbc0-f44c-57d5-999d-8e8824d858fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491135 #edb/ref 1074 "Substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ into the expression for $Q(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ax \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491154 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491154 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94071" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491154 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491154 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the quadratic form \n$Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+2{x}_{3}^{2}.$ \nEvaluate $Q(x)$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491154 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "64ee7b8f-3e22-5cf5-9caf-3d5b7abcf58b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491154 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given that \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the expression for $Q(x)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot (-3)^{2} \\\\  & =1+2+6+18 \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491163 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491163 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94121" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491163 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491163 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & b & c \\\\ 1 & c & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the quadratic form $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+4{x}_{2}^{2}-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-2{x}_{3}^{2}$ is given above. What is the value of $a+b+c?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491163 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "73743ec2-4a1f-56a6-9413-8c931a00dbaa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491163 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the matrix of the quadratic form must be symmetric, we rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+4{x}_{2}^{2}-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+0{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+1{x}_{1}{x}_{3} \\\\  & +\\,0{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}^{2}+(-4){x}_{2}{x}_{3} \\\\  & +\\,1{x}_{3}{x}_{1}+(-4){x}_{3}{x}_{2}+(-2){x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that to get a symmetric matrix, we must do the following splits:\n\n- we split the coefficient of $2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}$ between the entries ${a}_{13}={a}_{31}=\\frac{2}{2}=1,$ and\n\n- we split the coefficient of $-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}$ between the entries ${a}_{23}={a}_{32}=\\frac{-8}{2}=-4.$\n\nSo, the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=3,$ $b=4,$ and $c=-4.$ Finally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=3+4+(-4)=3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491171 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491171 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140930" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491171 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491171 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven the matrix $A$ above, evaluate the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491171 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "80dc5c66-c7d3-5e30-acc4-5e4e63237441" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491171 #edb/ref 1074 "Substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ into the expression for $Q(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ax \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
